      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Metformin 2550 mg three times daily is 7,650 mg/day, which is three times the maximum recommended daily dose. That raises the risk of severe lactic acidosis, and I don't know of a clinical situation where it would be appropriate, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals:** this totals 2,550 mg/day, the usual maximum. I can enter it now if you want.\n- **A lower dose:** if the patient is starting metformin, a typical start is 500 mg once or twice daily, titrated up. I can enter that instead.\n- **A dose you believe is intended:** if you have a specific reason for a higher dose, please talk to pharmacy first. I can enter it once pharmacy has approved it through your hospital's dose-override process.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it."
      }
